{#- Listen2 2.0 draft: pronunciation by meaning and sounds-like rules. Claims 21 to 23, plan section 5. No
    accuracy figure for words by meaning: none is in the fact sheet. -#}
{% from "components/listen2-2/slots.njk" import confirm %}
    <!-- Pronunciation -->
    <section id="pronunciation" style="max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 88px 24px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 56px; align-items: flex-start" aria-labelledby="l2-pron">
      <div style="flex: 1 1 460px; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px">
        <div style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #38B6FF">Pronunciation</div>
        <h2 id="l2-pron" style="margin: 0; font-size: clamp(32px, 4vw, 48px); line-height: 1.1; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.02em">Same spelling, different word</h2>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 19px; line-height: 1.6; color: #D4D4D0">English is full of words that change their sound with their meaning. You read a book yesterday and will read another tomorrow. A lead pipe has nothing to do with the lead in a play. Listen2 Voices now read these words by their meaning in the sentence, and an accented spelling such as résumé is read as the noun, not the verb.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: #A3A3A0">The setting is called Read Words by Their Meaning, under Pronunciation.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="flex: 1 1 420px; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px">
        <h3 style="margin: 0; font-size: 26px; line-height: 1.25; font-weight: 700">When a word still comes out wrong</h3>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: #A3A3A0">Names, place names and the words of your own field are the usual trouble spots. A Sounds Like rule fixes them: write the word the way it sounds, the way a newspaper spells out a name, hear a preview, and save it. Listen2 Voices say exactly the sounds you wrote, and every document follows the rule from then on.</p>
        <div style="border: 1px solid #2A2A2D; border-radius: 20px; background: #131315; padding: 22px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px">
          <div style="font-family: 'Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono', monospace; font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #A3A3A0">A sounds-like rule</div>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 21px; line-height: 1.5">Saoirse <span style="color: #A3A3A0">sounds like</span> SEER-shuh</p>
        </div>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: #A3A3A0">Not sure which letters make a sound? The Sound Key in the form shows what to type. Other voices follow the rule too, reading a plain-word version of your spelling.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
